{"recall": "Recall is a metric used in classification tasks to evaluate the performance of a model in identifying positive instances within a dataset. It is particularly important in scenarios where missing positive instances can have significant consequences.|Here is another description: Recall is a performance metric used to evaluate the effectiveness of a model in identifying all relevant instances within a dataset. It is one of the key metrics listed alongside Accuracy, Precision, and F1-Score in the context table."},
{"recall__is__proportion_of_correct_positive_samples_among_positive_samples_in_the_real_world": "The predicate \"is\" in this context signifies the definition or the meaning of the term \"Recall\" within the field of classification metrics. It establishes that Recall is specifically defined as a certain proportion."},
{"proportion_of_correct_positive_samples_among_positive_samples_in_the_real_world": "The proportion of correct positive samples among positive samples in the real world refers to the ratio of true positive instances that are correctly identified by the model out of all actual positive instances present in the dataset. This metric helps in understanding the model's ability to capture all relevant positive cases."},
{"tp": "TP, or True Positive, is a term used in the context of evaluating the performance of classification models. It represents the number of instances where the model correctly predicts a positive outcome, meaning the actual class of the instance is positive and the model's prediction is also positive."},
{"tp__means__true_positive": "The predicate \"means\" in this context serves to define or clarify the term TP. It indicates that TP is an abbreviation or shorthand for the phrase \"true positive,\" which is a specific outcome in classification tasks."},
{"true_positive": "True positive refers to the scenario in a classification model where both the actual class of an instance and the model's prediction for that instance are positive. It is one of the four possible outcomes in binary classification, alongside true negative, false positive, and false negative."},
{"tn": "TN is an abbreviation used in the context of evaluating classification models, specifically referring to one of the outcomes in a confusion matrix."},
{"tn__means__true_negative": "The term'means' in this context is used to define or explain the abbreviation TN, providing its full form and significance in the evaluation of classification models."},
{"true_negative": "True negative (TN) refers to the number of correct predictions where the actual outcome is negative and the model also predicts a negative outcome. It is one of the four fundamental metrics used in binary classification to assess the performance of a classification model."},
{"fp": "FP, or false positive, is a term used in classification metrics to describe instances where the model incorrectly predicts a positive outcome for a sample that is actually negative."},
{"fp__means__false_positive": "The term \"means\" in this context is used to define or explain the abbreviation FP, indicating that FP stands for false positive."},
{"false_positive": "False positive refers to a classification result where the model incorrectly identifies a negative sample as positive. This is an error in the model's predictions, contributing to a lower precision metric."},
{"fn": "FN, or false negative, is a term used in the context of evaluating classification models. It refers to instances where the model incorrectly predicts a negative outcome for a sample that is actually positive."},
{"fn__means__false_negative": "The predicate'means' in this context serves to define or explain the term FN, clarifying that it stands for false negative in the evaluation of classification models."},
{"false_negative": "False negative, abbreviated as FN, is a classification error where the model fails to identify a positive instance, incorrectly labeling it as negative. This can be critical in applications where missing positive cases have significant consequences."},
