{"disaster_data_classification_methods": "Disaster data classification methods refer to the techniques and approaches used to categorize and analyze data related to disasters. These methods are crucial for effective disaster management and response, as they help in organizing and interpreting the vast amount of information generated during and after a disaster."},
{"disaster_data_classification_methods__can_be_divided_into__two_categories": "The phrase \"can be divided into\" indicates a categorization or classification of the subject into distinct groups or types. In this context, it suggests that disaster data classification methods are organized into two main categories based on the type of data they utilize."},
{"two_categories": "The two categories of disaster data classification methods are those that use unimodal data and those that fuse multimodal data. Unimodal methods rely on a single type of data, such as text or images, while multimodal methods integrate multiple types of data to enhance analysis and accuracy."},
{"unimodal_data__only_use__one_type_of_data_for_analysis": "The predicate \"only use\" indicates a restriction in the analysis process to a singular data type. In disaster data classification, this means that the method relies solely on either text or image data, without integrating other types of data that could provide a more comprehensive understanding of the situation."},
