{"unimodal_data__can_be_further_divided_into__text_based_and_image_based_methods": "The phrase \"can be further divided into\" indicates a categorization or classification within the broader category of unimodal data. It suggests that unimodal data is not a monolithic entity but can be broken down into more specific types based on the nature of the data used."},
{"text_based_and_image_based_methods": "Text-based and image-based methods are the two specific types into which unimodal data can be divided. Text-based methods involve the collection and analysis of textual data from social media to extract information such as sentiment, demands, and rescue requirements. Image-based methods, on the other hand, utilize image data to classify and analyze disaster-related content using advanced machine learning techniques."},
{"multimodal_data_fusion_methods": "Multimodal data fusion methods refer to techniques that combine different types of data, such as text and images, for comprehensive analysis. These methods are particularly useful in disaster management for gaining situational awareness by integrating various data sources."},
{"multimodal_data_fusion_methods__usually_integrate__multiple_data_for_analysis": "The predicate \"usually integrate\" describes the common practice of multimodal data fusion methods, which involve combining and synthesizing multiple data types to enhance the analysis process. This integration aims to leverage the strengths of each data type to provide a more complete understanding of the situation."},
{"multiple_data_for_analysis": "Multiple data for analysis refers to the various types of data, such as text, images, and possibly other modalities like audio or video, that are combined in multimodal data fusion methods. These data types provide different perspectives and information, which, when integrated, can lead to more accurate and comprehensive disaster analysis."},
